Instruction: Read the following excerpt from a merger agreement and select the option that best represents the agreement.\n\nQuestion: Is the 'ability to consummate' concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement Excerpt: "'Material Adverse Effect' shall mean any event, condition, change or effect that, either on its own or in the aggregate with other events, has had or would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse effect on the Company’s business, results of operations, financial condition or prospects. This definition expressly includes any event or circumstance which would, or would reasonably be expected to, prevent, materially delay or impair the ability of the Company to consummate the transactions contemplated herein. Notwithstanding the foregoing, the following exceptions shall apply: (i) any adverse effect arising solely from changes in general economic or market-wide financial conditions; (ii) adverse effects resulting from fluctuations in interest rates, foreign exchange rates or similar capital market conditions; (iii) performance declines attributable exclusively to force majeure events or other natural disruptions, including pandemics and natural disasters; and (iv) effects that originate from routine internal forecast adjustments. However, no event or condition that impairs, delays or otherwise affects the ability to consummate the transaction shall be excluded from qualifying as a Material Adverse Effect under this definition."
Choices:

