Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.

Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement: "Aggregate Adverse Impact" shall mean any change, event, or circumstance that: 
   (i) materially diminishes the financial condition, operations, or assets of the Target Company;
   (ii) or would, in combination with any similar events, delay, hinder, or prevent the ability of the Target Company to consummate the closing of the transaction contemplated herein.

Notwithstanding the foregoing, with respect to the events described in subsection (i) only, no effect shall be deemed to have occurred if such event arises solely from: 
   (a) broad changes in general economic or market conditions; 
   (b) fluctuations in industry-specific trends; or 
   (c) other customary force majeure events, including changes in regulatory, legislative or natural disaster circumstances; 

provided that nothing in this exception shall apply to events described in subsection (ii) relating to the Target Company's ability to consummate the transaction, which shall remain fully actionable. Furthermore, any adverse impact resulting from a decline in third-party credit ratings or market liquidity shall only be taken into account if it demonstrates a disproportionate adverse effect compared to industry benchmarks.

